{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed581286445/2/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6ca952ad\" lines=\"15-30\">\n<description>\nDrop every large foreground object straight down to the bottom while leaving small marker objects fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a dominant background color and exactly one foreground color.\n- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.\n- Objects of area at most three are stationary markers; larger objects are moving sprites.\n\nRule:\n- Clear each moving sprite from its original position.\n- Translate each moving sprite vertically downward until its bbox bottom reaches the grid bottom.\n- Repaint each translated sprite in its original color; small markers stay unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Object area separates the active falling sprites from the small fixed markers, and all active sprites follow the same vertical path.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fc4aaf52\" lines=\"17-34\">\n<description>\nSplit the two-color object horizontally, swap its colors, and slide the upper half to the right.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n- All foreground pixels form one 4-connected object with an even-height bbox.\n- The object has foreground pixels on both rows adjacent to its horizontal midpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Split the object into upper and lower halves at the midpoint of its bbox.\n- Swap the two foreground colors in both halves.\n- Keep the lower half in place and shift the upper half right until its lower-left\n  boundary pixel is diagonally adjacent to the lower half's upper-right boundary pixel.\n\nKey insight:\n- The object is not copied or reflected; it is broken at the horizontal waist,\n  recolored by exchanging the two visible object colors, then rejoined diagonally.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e45ef808\" lines=\"31-46\">\n<description>\nMark the shallowest and deepest columns of a solid terrain boundary with vertical colored guide lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- The first row is a solid empty separator.\n- Below it, each column consists of upper fill cells followed by a contiguous suffix of terrain cells.\n- The bottom row is all terrain, so every column has a well-defined first terrain row.\n\nRule:\n- Find the column whose first terrain row is latest; it is unique and its upper fill run becomes maroon.\n- Find the rightmost column whose first terrain row is earliest; its upper fill run becomes yellow.\n- Preserve the empty separator, terrain cells, and all other upper fill cells.\n\nKey insight:\n- The task is a column-height summary of the terrain profile: color the deepest upper run and the highest terrain peak.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8f215267\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nCount same-color patterns per frame and represent the tally as dots inside.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Hollow rectangular frames in various colors.\n- Small monochrome pattern sprites scattered outside frames.\n\nRule:\n- Remove all small patterns from the grid.\n- For each frame of color C, count how many patterns share color C.\n- Draw that many dots along the frame's center row, spaced by 2, starting from the right.\n\nKey insight: Frames act as tally displays — scattered same-color patterns are \"absorbed\" and their count is shown as interior dots.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"79cce52d\" lines=\"29-44\">\n<description>\nCrop off the control row and column, then cyclically roll the payload by the two marker offsets.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is square, with a control row on top and a control column on the left.\n- The control row and column share one filler color and one single-pixel marker color.\n- The lower-right square is the payload and contains none of the control colors.\n\nRule\n- Remove the top control row and left control column.\n- Roll the remaining payload down by the marker's zero-based position in the left control column.\n- Roll the payload right by the marker's zero-based position in the top control row.\n\nKey insight\n- The two border markers are cyclic shift amounts for the payload, not colors to preserve.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"f560132c\" lines=\"259-273\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw pieces into a rectangle, recoloring each by a legend sprite's color map.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several monochrome non-bg sprites (the \"pieces\"), scattered across the grid.\n- One sprite is the legend: it contains a multi-color k×k patch (typically 2×2) encoding output colors.\n\nRule:\n- Tile all pieces (rotations/reflections allowed) into a tight rectangle with no gaps.\n- The legend's k×k color patch defines a spatial color map over the rectangle.\n- Each piece is recolored to the legend-map color corresponding to its position in the assembled rectangle.\n\nKey insight: The legend piece doubles as both a jigsaw piece and a color key — its internal k×k palette assigns colors by spatial quadrant.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"af726779\" lines=\"17-35\">\n<description>\nGrow an alternating pink/orange triangular trace downward from the horizontal orange seed row.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a green background and one horizontal row containing all orange foreground pixels.\n- The seed row may contain isolated orange pixels and adjacent orange runs.\n- Pink is absent from the input and is used for the first generated row.\n\nRule:\n- Copy the input grid.\n- Two rows below the current active row, place a child pixel between each active pair\n  whose middle cell in the current row is background.\n- Alternate generated row colors between pink and orange, stopping when no children\n  can be generated or the next row would leave the grid.\n\nKey insight:\n- Only an active-background-active triplet in the current row reproduces; contiguous\n  active runs do not produce children through occupied middle cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2b9ef948\" lines=\"51-65\">\n<description>\nMove the yellow 3x3 frame by the displacement encoded by the gray path, then draw its diagonal yellow corner rays.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is black.\n- One 3x3 yellow frame has a non-yellow control-color center.\n- A second control-color pixel is connected through gray path pixels to a lone yellow endpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Use the vector from the second control-color pixel to the lone yellow endpoint as the frame's translation.\n- Fill the whole output with the control color.\n- Draw the translated yellow frame and extend yellow diagonal rays outward from its four corners to the grid edge.\n\nKey insight: The gray path's turns are irrelevant; its two colored endpoints encode the net displacement to apply to the frame.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"c7f57c3e\" lines=\"22-35\">\n<description>\nSwap every sprite to the opposite prototype while preserving scale and position.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Two distinct small sprite patterns (prototypes) appear at various scales.\n- Each sprite instance is a scaled copy of one of the two prototypes, placed on the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the two prototype patterns from the smallest-area sprites.\n- For each sprite, determine which prototype it matches and its integer scale factor.\n- Replace it with the other prototype at the same scale, aligned by an internal anchor region (color-1 pixels).\n\nKey insight: Every sprite toggles to the alternate prototype; the anchor sub-region inside each sprite pins the alignment so swapped sprites land in the correct position regardless of scale.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f341894c\" lines=\"63-78\">\n<description>\nReorient each two-pixel blue/pink segment so it reads pink then blue when viewed from its border marker.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is dominant, with one border-only marker color and blue/pink foreground segments.\n- Every marker lies on the outer border and points inward along its row or column.\n- Along exactly one inward ray from each marker is a single adjacent two-pixel blue/pink segment.\n\nRule\n- For each marker, look inward perpendicular to the border side containing the marker.\n- Rewrite the two blue/pink pixels on that ray so the nearer one is pink and the farther one is blue.\n- Keep marker pixels, background pixels, and all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker gives a local viewing direction; the segment's required order is relative to that marker, not to global row or column order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e87109e9\" lines=\"68-83\">\n<description>\nEmit four teal beams from a square, turning at colored obstacles per a legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- Top band: grey (5) legend with colored vertical bars in cells; bar position (left or right) encodes turn direction.\n- Below: main grid with bg, a solid teal (8) square, and colored obstacle blocks.\n\nRule:\n- Remove the legend from the output.\n- From the teal square, shoot a beam in each cardinal direction (as a sliding copy of the square).\n- Each beam paints teal over bg cells as it moves.\n- On hitting an obstacle of color C, consult the legend: bar on left → turn counterclockwise, bar on right → turn clockwise. Continue in the new direction.\n- Beams stop at grid edges.\n\nKey insight: The legend's bar position (left vs right within its cell) is the encoding for turn direction at each obstacle color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"54dc2872\" lines=\"52-69\">\n<description>\nMove each marked L-shaped sprite onto the separate pixel matching its marker color.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground has 4-connected multicolor sprites and isolated one-pixel controls.\n- Each sprite has one repeated body color in an L shape plus one embedded marker\n  pixel of a different color.\n\nRule\n- If the embedded marker color also appears as a separate isolated control pixel,\n  translate the whole sprite so the body L-bend lands on that control pixel.\n- Clear each moved sprite from its old position, clear its consumed control pixel,\n  then paste all translated sprites; sprites without a matching control stay put.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker color pairs a sprite with its destination control, while the L-bend\n  gives the orientation-independent anchor cell to place on that destination.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a6953f00\" lines=\"45-63\">\n<description>\nExtract the top-left or top-right 2x2 corner crop selected by the top edge clue.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a square color grid whose answer is one of the two 2x2 crops\n  touching the top edge and a grid corner.\n- A repeated color on the top row, when present, acts as the primary side cue.\n- Otherwise, the two top corner colors have different whole-grid frequencies.\n\nRule\n- If a top-row color repeats, find the repeated top-row occurrence nearest to\n  a left or right edge and take the 2x2 top-corner crop on that side.\n- If the top row has no repeated color, compare the whole-grid frequencies of\n  the two top-corner colors and take the side whose corner color is rarer.\n\nKey insight\n- The task is not asking for an arbitrary 2x2 search: the decisive information\n  is on the top edge, and it selects which top corner crop to keep.\n</description>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
